  "situation": "The Soviet Union made concessions on Trieste and accepted the principle of free navigation on the Danube. It also agreed in principle to international inspection of armaments and to changing the treatment of abstentions in Security Council voting; arrangements were made for Foreign Ministers Deputies to meet before the Moscow Conference. At the same time, the United States and United Kingdom were assessed as firm, and further Soviet expansion by force was considered to carry a risk of war. Soviet agriculture faced serious conditions and some industries were missing plan quotas, while signs of public apathy and unrest were reported. Assessments also identified possible Soviet advantages in general disarmament and a need for support from smaller nations outside the Soviet bloc.",
